# Vault HTTP export and recycle integration test correction

The current runtime implements normal confirmed deletion as a move into an enabled native recycle bin. The native entry remains in the database, keeps its UUID, previous-parent UUID, history and unknown XML, and remains part of KDBX export. The active-entry API hides it. The old `app.integration.test.mjs` asserted that the total exported entry count became zero immediately after this move. That assertion belongs to permanent deletion, so it is obsolete for the accepted recycle behavior.

The unchanged production test was independently replayed: two tests, one pass and one failure, the same `1 !== 0` assertion at its final line. Exact source/test bytes, the new failed TAP, the parent's failed full-suite raw logs and its before/after source inventories are retained. The inherited full-suite execution is not counted as our own run. All 11 current runtime, host, storage, test and document bindings stayed exact before and after both owned runs.

The proposed file retains the complete first test body and every assertion before the old final deletion line. It replaces that line with stronger checks: the soft-delete response is explicitly recycled rather than permanent; active reads/history commands refuse the recycled entry; the sanitized bin list and readonly preview keep the correct identity and content; export retains exactly one native entry in the group identified by `RecycleBinUUID`, its exact previous-parent UUID, exact history XML and synthetic private metadata. Restarting the shared host instance from the same encrypted store retains those properties and begins locked.

`emptyRecycleBin` is deliberately unsupported in this entry-only slice, and its rejection must leave the encrypted record byte-identical. Unconfirmed and stale-revision permanent deletion must also leave it unchanged. Confirmed per-entry `recycleDelete` with `permanent:true` removes the entry from active, recycled and total exported projections and adds exactly one tombstone with the correct UUID and a valid decoded deletion time. Another host-instance restart retains that removal/tombstone and private database metadata. The fixture has one entry; the test does not claim group deletion or a general empty-bin command.

The corrected current-runtime run passes both tests, with no skips. The runnable work input differs from the production-ready proposal only in four import paths to the real shared host and native modules; these exact substitutions and hashes are recorded. The proposed file has ordinary production-relative imports. Runtime, UI, production files, previous receipt and original records remain unchanged; the parent is authorized to review and install only the proposed test.

The corrected two-test run creates six HTTP server instances in one test OS process: two in the unchanged CRUD test and four in the corrected KDBX test. It performs four host-instance close/restart transitions in total, three in the corrected test. These are not genuine OS host-process restart comparisons. The retained baseline run uses four server instances. No original program, assembly, browser, benchmark, or account was executed by this diagnosis. Earlier authenticated original comparisons remain separate evidence.

The source-only contract check preserves the exact GPL-2.0-or-later license and three released KeePass2.61.1 files. `PwDatabase.cs` defaults recycle enablement to true (lines87 and563). `MainForm_Functions.cs` lines5168–5188 distinguishes permanent deletion/tombstones from moving the same entry and setting its previous parent. `KdbxFile.Write.cs` lines360–419 traverses the database group tree for output; entry `PreviousParentGroup` is written only for KDBX4.1 at lines545–546. The candidate fixture/export is KDBX4.1; this new test does not rebind the preserved original default4.0 previous-parent-loss boundary or claim default-release equality. It is source-assisted diagnosis and candidate HTTP integration, not a new original runtime comparison or full product parity.

The owned runner caps each output stream at1MiB, uses a60-second child deadline, removes inherited `NODE_TEST_CONTEXT`, and authenticates current source identities before and after. The tests use isolated synthetic temporary directories and their existing checked cleanup hooks. Generic Windows descendant isolation and hard filesystem deadlines are not claimed. Full product, security and superiority claims remain false.
